.TH aaltitoad-ls 1 2026-10-18 "version v1.2.1" AALTITOAD

.SH NAME
aaltitoad-ls \- tick tock automata language server for graphedit

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aaltitoad-ls
[\fB\-h\fR]
[\fB\-V\fR]
[\fB\-v\fI level\fR]

.SH DESCRIPTION
This program is the language server of the NTTA models of graphedit (see tta(7)). graphedit, with its NTTA plugin
(graphedit\-ntta), runs it when the environment variable \fBAALTITOAD_LANGUAGE_SERVER\fR names it, and speaks the graph
language server protocol of graphedit with it: JSON\-RPC messages with content lengths on the standard input and output.
The program is not meant to be run by hand.

The server reads the models of the project (the model files, and the open models as they are edited), builds their
network as aaltitoad would run it, and reports what keeps aaltitoad from running it (the \fBAALTITOAD\-...\fR
diagnostics), for example guards that cannot be parsed, subcomponents that do not match their templates and variables
that are used but not declared. Models without vertices declare the variables of the environment
(\fBextern bool button := false\fR). It also debugs networks (see \fBDEBUGGING\fR) and answers queries about them (see
\fBQUERIES\fR).

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
show help message and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
show version number and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbosity " " \fIlevel
set the verbosity of the log on the standard error to \fIlevel\fR (6 for max verbosity, default 3: warnings).

.SH DEBUGGING
A debug session starts a runtime that stops before its first tick. When a tick can take several sets of edges, those
sets are the choices of the step. Breakpoints go on locations and edges, with optional conditions and hit counts.
While the runtime is stopped, variables can be changed, instances can be put into other locations and earlier states
can be restored. Instances are named after their scope, e.g. \fBMain.lamp(1)\fR.

The runtime has no tockers: the environment only changes when its variables are changed in the debugger, and clocks and
timers do not advance by themselves. When no edge is enabled, the runtime stops until a variable changes. The arguments
of a debug configuration change how it runs:
.TP
.B policy
how the runtime chooses between the choices of a step: \fBfirst\fR (the default) or \fBrandom\fR.
.TP
.B seed
the seed of the random choices, so that random runs can be repeated.
.TP
.B delay
milliseconds between two steps when the runtime runs (default 0: as fast as possible).
.TP
.B history
how many states can be restored (default 1000).

.SH QUERIES
The server answers the queries of graphedit (the Queries panel, \fBgraphedit\-cli verify\fR and the \fBverify.*\fR
methods of its agent api) in the computation tree logic (CTL) of verifier(1):
.TP
.BI "E F " p
can a state in which \fIp\fR holds be reached? (also written \fBE<>\fR \fIp\fR). If it can, the result has a trace to
such a state (a witness).
.TP
.BI "A G " p
does \fIp\fR hold in every state that can be reached? (also written \fBA[]\fR \fIp\fR). If it does not, the result has a
trace to a state in which \fIp\fR does not hold (a counterexample).
.PP
The predicate \fIp\fR is an expression over the variables of the network, or a location id. Private variables are named
after their instance (\fBMain.lamp(1).on\fR). Locations are named \fIinstance\fB@\fIlocation\fR in expressions, by
their nickname or their id, since the dot already names the private variables:
\fBE F Main.lamp(1)@on && !button.1\fR asks whether the instance \fBMain.lamp(1)\fR can be in its location \fBon\fR
while \fBbutton.1\fR is false.

The queries are answered with the forward reachability search of verifier(1): it searches the states that the network
reaches by its ticks and by the changes of its environment that the interesting tocker finds (values of the external
variables that make the guards of the current locations hold or not), until a state answers the query or every
reachable state was searched. The environment may also stay as it is after a tick. The traces are not necessarily the
shortest ones. A trace is shown in the debugger of graphedit, with the changes of the environment as steps of the kind
\fIinput\fR.

The search runs step by step, so that the server keeps reading messages: a query can be cancelled, and the server
reports how many states it searched. It answers one query at a time. The option \fBmaxStates\fR of a query (default
100000) bounds the search: a query whose answer needs more states is answered \fIunknown\fR.

.SH EXAMPLES
Edit the models of a project in graphedit, with aaltitoad\-ls as the language server of its NTTA models:
.PP
.RS
AALTITOAD_LANGUAGE_SERVER="aaltitoad\-ls \-\-verbosity 4" graphedit my\-project/
.RE
.PP
Answer the queries of the project on the command line (the exit code is 1 unless all of them hold):
.PP
.RS
AALTITOAD_LANGUAGE_SERVER=aaltitoad\-ls graphedit\-cli \-\-project my\-project/ verify
.RE

